 <!-- Certification Verification Guide -->
 <section id="cert-verification" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-4">3. Certification Verification: How to Check If Certs Are Real</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Listing certifications on a website is easy. Verifying they are real requires knowing where to look. Many suppliers display expired certificates, certs belonging to other companies, or test reports that do not constitute full certification. Here is how to verify the most common certifications independent of the supplier's claims:</p>
 <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
 <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
 <thead>
 <tr class="bg-brandBlue text-white text-[11px] font-black uppercase tracking-widest">
 <th class="p-4 text-left">Certification</th>
 <th class="p-4 text-left">What It Covers</th>
 <th class="p-4 text-left">How to Verify</th>
 </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody class="text-slate-600">
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4 font-bold">ISO 9001</td><td class="p-4">Quality management system</td><td class="p-4">Check cert number on the issuing body's database (SGS, TÜV, Bureau Veritas)</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100 bg-slate-50"><td class="p-4 font-bold">FCC</td><td class="p-4">US electromagnetic compatibility</td><td class="p-4">Search the FCC ID on <a href="https://fcc.gov/oet/ea/fccid" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brandBlue hover:underline">fcc.gov</a>, if the ID doesn't exist, the cert is fake</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4 font-bold">CE</td><td class="p-4">EU safety &amp; EMC compliance</td><td class="p-4">Request the Declaration of Conformity and verify the notified body number on the EU NANDO database</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100 bg-slate-50"><td class="p-4 font-bold">UL</td><td class="p-4">North American product safety</td><td class="p-4">Look up the UL file number on <a href="https://productspec.ul.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brandBlue hover:underline">productspec.ul.com</a></td></tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4 font-bold">Qi (wireless)</td><td class="p-4">WPC wireless charging standard</td><td class="p-4">Verify the Qi ID on the <a href="https://www.wirelesspowerconsortium.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-brandBlue hover:underline">WPC database</a></td></tr>
 <tr class="border-b border-slate-100 bg-slate-50"><td class="p-4 font-bold">UN38.3</td><td class="p-4">Battery transport safety</td><td class="p-4">Request the UN38.3 test summary from a certified lab (required for all lithium battery shipments)</td></tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-brandBlue/5 border-l-4 border-brandOrange p-5 rounded-r-xl">
 <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm"><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> A legitimate China charger supplier maintains current certifications with matching model numbers. WOWOHCOOL provides certificate copies and test reports on request, and welcomes third-party verification. If a supplier hesitates to share certificate numbers or issuing body details, consider it a red flag and escalate your due diligence.</p>
